# Viewing, Listing, and Editing Files in Azure Blob Storage using PowerShell and Azure CLI

Here's how to work with Azure Blob Storage containers using a service account with both PowerShell and Azure CLI:

## Prerequisites
- Azure service principal (service account) with appropriate permissions (Storage Blob Data Contributor or similar)
- Azure PowerShell module or Azure CLI installed

## Using Azure PowerShell

### 1. Authenticate with Service Principal
```powershell
$tenantId = "<your-tenant-id>"
$appId = "<your-service-principal-app-id>"
$secret = "<your-service-principal-secret>"

$secureSecret = ConvertTo-SecureString -String $secret -AsPlainText -Force
$credential = New-Object -TypeName System.Management.Automation.PSCredential -ArgumentList $appId, $secureSecret

Connect-AzAccount -ServicePrincipal -Credential $credential -Tenant $tenantId
```

### 2. List Blobs in a Container
```powershell
$storageAccountName = "<your-storage-account>"
$containerName = "<your-container>"
$resourceGroup = "<resource-group-name>"

$storageContext = New-AzStorageContext -StorageAccountName $storageAccountName -UseConnectedAccount

Get-AzStorageBlob -Container $containerName -Context $storageContext | Select-Object Name, LastModified, Length
```

### 3. Download a Blob to View/Edit
```powershell
$blobName = "example.txt"
$destinationPath = "C:\temp\example.txt"

Get-AzStorageBlobContent -Container $containerName -Blob $blobName -Destination $destinationPath -Context $storageContext
```

### 4. Upload an Edited Blob
```powershell
Set-AzStorageBlobContent -Container $containerName -File $destinationPath -Blob $blobName -Context $storageContext -Force
```

## Using Azure CLI

### 1. Authenticate with Service Principal
```bash
az login --service-principal -u <your-service-principal-app-id> -p <your-service-principal-secret> --tenant <your-tenant-id>
```

### 2. List Blobs in a Container
```bash
az storage blob list \
    --account-name <your-storage-account> \
    --container-name <your-container> \
    --auth-mode login \
    --output table
```

### 3. Download a Blob to View/Edit
```bash
az storage blob download \
    --account-name <your-storage-account> \
    --container-name <your-container> \
    --name example.txt \
    --file ~/Downloads/example.txt \
    --auth-mode login
```

### 4. Upload an Edited Blob
```bash
az storage blob upload \
    --account-name <your-storage-account> \
    --container-name <your-container> \
    --name example.txt \
    --file ~/Downloads/example.txt \
    --auth-mode login \
    --overwrite
```

## Notes
1. For editing, you'll need to download the file, edit it locally, then upload it back
2. For text files, you can use `Get-Content` (PowerShell) or `cat` (CLI) to view contents directly
3. Ensure your service principal has the appropriate RBAC roles assigned (Storage Blob Data Contributor at minimum)
4. For large files or frequent operations, consider using Azure Storage Explorer with service principal credentials

